WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.111
[soft ambient music]

00:00:03.111 --> 00:00:07.000
Chess never needed sight.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.202
Forty-three million people are blind.

00:00:10.202 --> 00:00:13.317
Two hundred ninety-five million live

00:00:13.317 --> 00:00:16.000
with serious visual impairment.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.519
Chess is one of the very few games

00:00:18.519 --> 00:00:21.185
they play on completely equal terms.

00:00:21.185 --> 00:00:24.000
The game itself needs no sight at all.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.388
Then you open a chess app.

00:00:25.388 --> 00:00:27.204
Every mainstream platform is built

00:00:27.204 --> 00:00:28.806
around a drag-and-drop board -

00:00:28.806 --> 00:00:30.301
so a screen reader reads out

00:00:30.301 --> 00:00:31.529
sixty-four empty cells.

00:00:31.529 --> 00:00:33.612
The hard part stops being your opponent

00:00:33.612 --> 00:00:35.000
and becomes the interface.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.124
And every workaround costs you something.

00:00:37.124 --> 00:00:38.938
A helper who has to be in the room.

00:00:38.938 --> 00:00:40.596
A braille board that can't reach

00:00:40.596 --> 00:00:41.796
anyone online.

00:00:41.321 --> 00:00:43.342
Or a text interface that makes you hold

00:00:43.342 --> 00:00:45.000
the whole position in your head.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.500
[music swells]

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:52.000
VoiceChessmate

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.304
You hold one key and say the move.

00:00:56.304 --> 00:01:00.481
It plays, answers, and explains -

00:01:00.481 --> 00:01:02.000
out loud.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.595
The moves are spoken in the IBCA

00:01:04.595 --> 00:01:06.216
tournament alphabet.

00:01:06.216 --> 00:01:08.324
Anna, Bella, Cesar, David,

00:01:08.324 --> 00:01:10.514
Eva, Felix, Gustav, Hector.

00:01:10.514 --> 00:01:13.027
It's the notation blind players

00:01:13.027 --> 00:01:14.000
already use.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.200
Here's why that matters.

00:01:15.188 --> 00:01:16.970
B, D, E and G sound almost identical

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:18.170
over a microphone -

00:01:17.911 --> 00:01:19.446
and that's exactly the alphabet

00:01:19.446 --> 00:01:20.646
chess uses.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.168
One misheard letter isn't an error message.

00:01:22.168 --> 00:01:24.000
It's a legal, different, losing move.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.785
You can also just ask.

00:01:26.785 --> 00:01:30.329
Anything about the position,

00:01:30.329 --> 00:01:34.000
answered from the real board.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.187
And when you've done something stupid,

00:01:37.187 --> 00:01:39.452
you can ask about that too.

00:01:39.452 --> 00:01:43.310
That answer isn't a language model's opinion -

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:47.000
it's the engine's real evaluation, in pawns.

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Underneath: audio streams as PCM sixteen

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.600
over a WebSocket to AssemblyAI's

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:51.800
Voice Agent API -

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:53.000
speech-to-text, turn detection,

00:01:53.000 --> 00:01:54.650
the language model and the voice,

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:55.850
on one connection.

00:01:55.550 --> 00:01:57.400
It acts through eighteen typed tools,

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:59.000
run against a real chess engine.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.008
And when it isn't sure, it doesn't guess.

00:02:01.008 --> 00:02:03.212
It reads back the real legal options instead.

00:02:03.212 --> 00:02:05.563
A sighted player undoes a wrong move instantly -

00:02:05.563 --> 00:02:07.131
a blind player might not find it

00:02:07.131 --> 00:02:08.331
for several moves.

00:02:08.012 --> 00:02:09.824
So the rule is: prefer making no move

00:02:09.824 --> 00:02:11.000
over making a wrong one.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:12.916
Which turns out not to be only for people

00:02:12.916 --> 00:02:14.116
who can't see the board.

00:02:14.037 --> 00:02:15.720
Blindfold play is a discipline every

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:16.981
serious club player trains.

00:02:16.981 --> 00:02:19.084
Hands-free means you can play while you walk.

00:02:19.084 --> 00:02:21.000
And you can just listen to a famous game.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.746
It's live, open source,

00:02:23.746 --> 00:02:29.000
and covered by six hundred and twelve tests.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:33.167
Chess never needed sight.

00:02:33.167 --> 00:02:38.000
Its software just assumed it.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:39.592
[music fades]

00:02:39.592 --> 00:02:44.000
voicechessmate - built on AssemblyAI
